Instead of storing opening brackets, this version stores the closing bracket that must come next. After seeing `(`, the stack gets `)`. This makes each closing bracket a direct comparison.

The array is used as a small manual stack, so no library stack is needed. A closing bracket is invalid when the stack is empty or when it does not match the top value.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isValid(String s) {
        char[] expected = new char[s.length()];
        int top = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char current = s.charAt(i);

            if (current == '(') {
                expected[top++] = ')';
            } else if (current == '[') {
                expected[top++] = ']';
            } else if (current == '{') {
                expected[top++] = '}';
            } else {
                if (top == 0 || expected[top - 1] != current) {
                    return false;
                }
                top--;
            }
        }

        return top == 0;
    }
}
```

The input string is not changed. A string such as `"(("` leaves two expected closers in the stack, so it correctly returns `false`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(n)`

The stack keeps the opening brackets that have not been closed yet. On every closing bracket, the last opening bracket is checked. This also catches cases like `([)]`, where every character exists but the nesting order is wrong.

A dry run for `"([])"` gives: push `(`, push `[`, pop `[`, then pop `(`. The stack is empty at the end, so the string is valid.

```javascript
class Solution {
    isValid(s) {
        const stack = [];
        const matching = {
            ")": "(",
            "]": "[",
            "}": "{"
        };

        for (const char of s) {
            if (char === "(" || char === "[" || char === "{") {
                stack.push(char);
            } else {
                if (stack.length === 0 || stack.pop() !== matching[char]) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        return stack.length === 0;
    }
}
```

The important edge cases are an empty string, a string beginning with a closing bracket, and leftover opening brackets. All are handled by the empty-stack checks and the final length check.

Time complexity: `O(n)`
Space complexity: `O(n)`

## Intuition

The wildcard character `'*'` can act as an opening parenthesis `'('`, a closing parenthesis `')'`, or an empty string. Instead of deciding its role immediately, we postpone the decision by storing the positions of all `'*'` characters. We also store the positions of all unmatched `'('` characters. Whenever we encounter a closing parenthesis `')'`, we first try to match it with the nearest unmatched `'('`. If no `'('` is available, we use a previously encountered `'*'` as an opening parenthesis. After processing the entire string, any remaining unmatched `'('` can still be matched with `'*'` characters that appear **after** them, treating those `'*'` as closing parentheses.

---

## Approach

1. Create two stacks:

   * `par` to store the indices of unmatched `'('`.
   * `star` to store the indices of `'*'`.

2. Traverse the string from left to right:

   * If the current character is `'('`, push its index onto `par`.
   * If the current character is `'*'`, push its index onto `star`.
   * If the current character is `')'`:

     * If `par` is not empty, pop one `'('` to match it.
     * Otherwise, if `star` is not empty, pop one `'*'` and treat it as `'('`.
     * If neither stack has an element, the string is invalid, so return `false`.

3. After the traversal, there may still be unmatched `'('`.

   * While both stacks are non-empty:

     * If the top `'*'` occurs **after** the top `'('`, use that `'*'` as `')'` and pop both stacks.
     * Otherwise, the `'('` cannot be matched, so return `false`.

4. If all `'('` have been matched (`par` is empty), return `true`; otherwise, return `false`.

---

## Complexity Analysis

* **Time Complexity:** **O(n)**, where `n` is the length of the string. Each character is pushed and popped at most once.
* **Space Complexity:** **O(n)**, due to the two stacks used to store indices of `'('` and `'*'`.


## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    bool isValid(string s) {
        stack<int> par;
        stack<int> star;

        int n = s.size();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            if (s[i] == '(') {
                par.push(i);
            }
            else if (s[i] == '*') {
                star.push(i);
            }
            else {

                if (!par.empty()) {
                    par.pop();
                }
                else if (!star.empty()) {
                    star.pop();
                }
                else {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }


        while (!par.empty() && !star.empty()) {

            if (par.top() < star.top()) {
                par.pop();
                star.pop();
            }
            else {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return par.empty();
    }
};
```

